  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 248 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nThe trough has closed northern and southern stream components \nto it, which look to merge together over the region Monday \nnight. Ahead of then, the southern feature will mature as it \nejects into the Plains. The midlevel reflection of the jet will \nround the trough base and cover the warm sector Monday \nafternoon. 700mb temperatures have trended warmer in southern \nIowa, so lapse rates are closer to 7 C/km. MUCAPE values could \nsurpass 500 J/kg in far southern Iowa. A line of storms will \nform along the cold front, but its threats will depend on how \nmuch clearing can take place ahead of its arrival. REFS ensemble\nsoundings and hodographs have favorable shear parameters for \ndamaging winds and tornadoes, but are at the mercy of the lower \nthermal profile. There is not much spread in the PBL temperature\nvalues, which struggle to mix in most all ensemble members. \nWithout near-surface instability, the threat will be mainly \nisolated damaging winds.\n\nAnother notable trend is the timing of the front arriving in \nIowa. This is because the two aforementioned components of the \ntrough will be merging together, causing the resultant closed \nlow to stall. The delayed arrival means severe weather chances \nare lower, since storms would be arriving after dark. The slowed\nmovement then increases the rainfall amounts, as the front will\nutilize the 1.5\" PWATs and drop 1-2\" in southern Iowa. \nSomething else we'll need to watch is storm potential in eastern\nIowa on Tuesday afternoon due to the front's late exit. That \nsaid, the thermal profile looks less impressive on Tuesday, by \ncomparison. Cooler temperatures will advect in on the backside. \nPrecipitation chances linger through much of the week due to the\nTROWAL of the system.",
